Q: Approaching the limit on an implicit function via bisection A: <p>There's nothing built in to the framework for this, you will need to roll your own, for instance:</p>
<pre><code>public static class NumericExtensions
{
    public static double Bisect(Func&lt;double, bool&gt; func, double xFalse, double xTrue, double tolerance = 1e-3, int maxIterations = 100)
    {
        if (!TryBisect(func, xFalse, xTrue, tolerance, maxIterations, out var boundary))
            throw new SolutionNotFoundException(); // Replace with your preferred exception
        return boundary;
    }

    public static bool TryBisect(Func&lt;double, bool&gt; func, double xFalse, double xTrue, double tolerance, int maxIterations, out double boundary)
    {
        if (tolerance &lt;= double.Epsilon)
            throw new ArgumentException($&quot;Invalid tolerance {tolerance}.&quot;);
        if (func(xFalse) || !func(xTrue))
            throw new ArgumentException(&quot;bad bracketing&quot;); // Or return false?
        boundary = 0.0;
        for (int i = 0; i &lt;= maxIterations; i++)
        {
            boundary = 0.5*xFalse + 0.5*xTrue; // Do not simplify to 0.5*(xFalse + xTrue) in case double.MinValue and double.MaxValue were passed in.
            if (Math.Abs(xTrue - xFalse) &lt; tolerance)
                return true;
            if (func(boundary))
                xTrue = boundary;
            else
                xFalse = boundary;
        }
        return false;
    }
}

public class SolutionNotFoundException : Exception
{
    public SolutionNotFoundException() : this(&quot;No solution found&quot;) { }
    public SolutionNotFoundException(string message) : base(message) { }
    public SolutionNotFoundException(string message, Exception innerException) : base(message, innerException) { }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Demo fiddle <a href="https://dotnetfiddle.net/yTtVCE" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a>.</p>
<p>That being said, in your question, you implied you had some <code>double</code> valued function, say a <code>Func&lt;double, double&gt; function</code> and needed to find where it crosses some value:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I will need to assume that the function is monotonic in its return value but may be increasing or decreasing: that is to say, for the value of <code>x</code> I need to find, the return value is <code>true</code> for all <code>x</code> above it and false for all <code>x</code> below it or it could be the inverted version of that.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>By converting your <code>function</code> from one returning a <code>double</code> into one returning a <code>bool</code>, you are essentially converting a continuous function to a discontinuous one, which actually makes your task harder.  Rather than that, if you retain your <code>double</code>-valued function and think of your problem as a <strong>root-finding</strong> problem like so:</p>
<pre><code>Func&lt;double, double&gt; f = x =&gt; function(x) - goalValue;
</code></pre>
<p>You should be able to use any off-the-shelf root finding package.  For instance, <a href="https://numerics.mathdotnet.com/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Math.NET Numerics</a> has the <a href="https://numerics.mathdotnet.com/api/MathNet.Numerics.RootFinding/index.htm" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>RootFinding</code></a> namespace which includes a <a href="https://github.com/mathnet/mathnet-numerics/blob/master/src/Numerics/RootFinding/Bisection.cs" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Bisection</code></a> algorithm:</p>
<pre><code>var root = MathNet.Numerics.RootFinding.Bisection.FindRoot(
    x =&gt; function(x) - goalValue, xMin, xMax, tolerance);
</code></pre>
<p>Do note one difference between this approach and the previous.  In the previous approach, the tolerance applies to the <strong>domain</strong> of the <code>IsValid(double x)</code> function, and specifies an acceptable tolerance for the input argument <code>x</code>.  In the second approach, the <code>tolerance</code> applies to the <strong>range</strong> of the <code>Func&lt;double, double&gt; function</code> and requires that the value of the function be within the tolerance of the goal value at the specified root.</p>
